1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
*music*

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,000
*music*

3
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Well, good morning, church.

4
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Good morning.

5
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Whew.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Good morning, church.

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
If we haven't had the privilege of meeting yet,

8
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,000
my name is Ryan,

9
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
and I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here,

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
and normally I try and carry this standout by myself,

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
but I didn't want to embarrass myself in front of new people,

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
so I had help.

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Happy Resurrection Day.

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Again, we're so glad you've joined us,

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
and I'm just believing, I've been praying all week,

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
that God has something for each and every one of us,

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,000
no matter how we brought,

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
no matter how many we came into this room,

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
no matter what is going on in our lives

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
or where we're at on our spiritual journey,

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
I just believe that the Holy Spirit is present

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
and wants to do something in each of our lives.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
So today we celebrate, we reflect, and we wrestle with the most consequential day in human history.

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The claims of the resurrection of Jesus Christ have quite literally turned the world upside down.

25
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Most of us have the whole month of December blocked off in our calendars because of Christmas.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Christmas does not exist without Easter.

27
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
No one is celebrating the birth of a crucified Jewish rabbi from 2,000 years ago.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
We celebrate the birth because of the resurrection.

29
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Everything that Christians claim and hold onto, millions of people have given their lives in martyrdom

30
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
for the sake of the belief that Jesus rose from the dead.

31
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
And we have to do something with that.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
All of us have to do something with the claims of the resurrection.

33
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
They demand our attention, they invite us to a response.

34
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Famously, C.S. Lewis has said, "If Jesus didn't raise from the dead,

35
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
He's either a lunatic, a liar, or Lord."

36
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Those are our only options.

37
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
And so we who come here and celebrate and sing and dance and jump around about an empty tomb,

38
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
we are a people to be of utmost pitied if the resurrection didn't happen.

39
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And if it did, then nothing is the same.

40
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
That's what today is all about.

41
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
So let's pray and invite God's Spirit to direct our attention where He wants it

42
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
and to produce the response that He intends for each one of us this morning.

43
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Holy Spirit, we invite you.

44
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,000
You're already here.

45
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
We give you freedom to do what you want to do in this room today.

46
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
We give you freedom to do what only you can do in the hearts and lives of each person in this room.

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
God, I invite faith to increase in the room this morning for people who are skeptical,

48
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
who are struggling, who are suffering, who have put their hope in you and have found it wanting.

49
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
God, may today you restore and renew faith.

50
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
May you strengthen faith in those of us that are holding tightly to you.

51
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
And for those of us who have turned our backs on you, God, may you just gently,

52
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
gently poke us on the shoulder and invite us to come back to the place that we were made to be.

53
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
I pray all of this in Jesus' name.

54
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Amen.

55
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
If you have your Bibles and you want to make sure I'm not making anything up this morning,

56
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
you can turn to Luke chapter 24.

57
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Matthew, Mark, and Luke start the New Testament.

58
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
So if you find the big header for the New Testament, turn right, go a couple pages,

59
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
you'll find Luke chapter 24.

60
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And this is one of, this is by far my favorite post-resurrection encounter.

61
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
It's one of my favorite stories in all of Scripture.

62
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
And there are a number of pertinent biblical passages about the resurrection of Jesus,

63
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
but I prayerfully selected this one, one, because it's my favorite and I get to pick.

64
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
So there's that.

65
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,000
But also because I believe that many of us in the room can directly relate to the characters in our story this morning.

66
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:37,000
So we're going to get to the story in just a minute, but for some context, Luke, the author of the gospel of Luke, a Gentile doctor,

67
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
he is crescendoing his entire account of Jesus in this march towards Jerusalem.

68
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Jesus has taught with authority.

69
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
He's done many miracles for three years.

70
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
He's gathered many disciples and preached to them that the kingdom of God has drawn near.

71
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Many of them have believed they've witnessed, they've witnessed his miracles and they are believing that he is the fulfillment of the prophet promises of the Old Testament Scriptures.

72
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And all of this is, is marching towards Jerusalem.

73
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
All of it is, is crescendoing in Jerusalem.

74
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
And we, and Jesus gets to Jerusalem and many of the crowds around last week are chanting, "Hosanna, Messiah, save now."

75
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
They're believing that he is the promised Messiah, urging him to do the things that he's been teaching about and demonstrating for years.

76
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Do the thing, set God's people free from their oppression.

77
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Bring God's presence and eternal reign to Jerusalem once and for all.

78
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
This is the day that we have been waiting for.

79
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:42,000
And within a day, I'm going to put my iPad on, do not disturb.

80
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Real people, real lives, welcome to church.

81
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Should have used paper on Easter, I should have known better.

82
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Within a day, all of their hopes and dreams are dashed.

83
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Jesus is handed over by the Jewish religious leaders to Rome and he is crucified.

84
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Many of his followers immediately scatter, not wanting to suffer the consequences of being associated with a crucified rebellion leader.

85
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
I mean, within an instant, they're gone, they're scattered, they're running away.

86
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
They don't want to be associated with this movement anymore.

87
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Everything they had put their hope into the last three years is gone.

88
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
It's dashed.

89
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
But some stayed together and tried to sort out what it could all be about.

90
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:33,000
They remember glimpses of Jesus talking about suffering, dying and raising from the dead,

91
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
but they didn't have any compartment in their brain to understand what that could possibly mean.

92
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
And so now they're gathered together in a room together, just trying to figure out what they're supposed to do now.

93
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And a group of women from this remnant, they go to the tomb as soon as they can on Sunday morning to take care of the body of the one whom they have come to love.

94
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
They are met by an empty tomb, angels telling them that he is risen from the dead, just as he said.

95
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
They say, go and tell the other followers.

96
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
So they do, but no one believes them because why would you?

97
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:15,000
I think we tend to distance ourselves from the ancients thinking that they must have had some idea about people.

98
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
They just thought people raised from the dead all the time.

99
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
No, resurrection was just as crazy to them as it is to us.

100
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
They were not expecting a dead person to come out of the grave.

101
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
This is not a regular occurrence.

102
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
And so they don't believe.

103
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
A claim of a resurrection would have been outlandish.

104
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
And this is where we pick up our story.

105
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
In Luke chapter 24, two, two of the followers who had stuck around for a couple of days,

106
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
who had hung out with the crew of disciples who were trying to figure out what in the world was going on.

107
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,000
The world was going on.

108
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
They had stuck around for a few days.

109
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
They had heard the testimony that these women found the tomb empty.

110
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
And yet their response is to walk away.

111
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Verse 13.

112
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Now that same day, on resurrection day, two of these disciples who had stuck around for a couple of days,

113
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
just trying to figure out what in the world was going on.

114
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Two of them decided to walk away from Jerusalem, the place that God had invited them to be.

115
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
They were walking away from the place God invited them to be to a place called Emmaus, about seven miles from Jerusalem.

116
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,000
And they were talking with each other about everything that had happened.

117
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And as they walked and discussed these things with each other, Jesus himself came up and walked alongside of them.

118
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:32,000
So two individuals from this group, they walk away from the place that Jesus has invited them to.

119
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Their lives have been turned upside down.

120
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Everything they were working towards.

121
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Everything they had put their hope in.

122
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Everything they had devoted their lives to.

123
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
Their friend, brutally murdered.

124
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
They've been told that he's raised from the dead, but that sounds crazy.

125
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Like possibly a mix of a trauma response and sleeplessness from these women who are suffering.

126
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:54,000
None of it makes sense.

127
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:59,000
And I just want to ask the question, how many of us can relate to this story?

128
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:06,000
We live in the part of, in a part of the United States where there's a church on every corner.

129
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:13,000
My guess is that pretty much all of us in the room have some proximity to the message and teachings of Jesus.

130
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
We have some understanding of who he claims to be.

131
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,000
And my guess is that many of us, if not most of us, probably want to believe the good news of Jesus.

132
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
We want a God that forgives our mistakes.

133
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
We want abundant and peaceful life that he talked about.

134
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:36,000
We want a hope that extends beyond the great.

135
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
But life happens.

136
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Suffering comes into our lives.

137
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
Life doesn't turn out the way that we thought it would.

138
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:56,000
We're often dazed and confused, suffering the pain of this world and skeptical that anything a Jewish rabbi from 2,000 years ago said or did could really have any impact on our lives today.

139
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:05,000
Can any of us resonate with these two disciples who just, in suffering and skepticism, chose to walk away?

140
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,000
I don't know about you, but I feel like I've lived this reality in my life more than a time or two.

141
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:21,000
I've been following Jesus for most of my life, going to the places that he's telling me to go, doing with my best intention the things that he's telling me to do.

142
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
And yet things haven't always worked out the way that I thought they would.

143
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Suffering often leads to skepticism, and I've been tempted to walk away more than a few times.

144
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:40,000
And I'm guessing that most of us in the room have had similar experiences at least once in our life, and maybe we're in that situation today.

145
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Which leads me to my primary question that I want to ask today of this text and of the Holy Spirit.

146
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:52,000
What does the resurrected Jesus do for those who are suffering and skeptical of him?

147
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:58,000
What does he do for people who are walking away from the place that he's invited them to be?

148
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:07,000
What does he do for people who are trying to build their life totally apart from his existence and his will for their lives?

149
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:16,000
What does the resurrected Jesus do for us when we're suffering and skeptical that anything he said or did could have any impact on our lives today?

150
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:32,000
Verse 15, as they talked and discussed these things with each other, Jesus himself came up and walked along with them, but they were kept from recognizing him.

151
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:40,000
First thing I want us to see in this passage is that Jesus, the resurrected Jesus, draws near to his suffering and skeptical friends.

152
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
They've turned their back on him, they've walked away from the direction that he's invited them to go.

153
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:51,000
We cannot just breeze past this reality.

154
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Jesus just fulfilled all of the law and prophets.

155
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:00,000
He just went to battle against the spiritual forces of darkness in the world.

156
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
He just conquered sin, death, and the grave.

157
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:08,000
I don't know about you, but that sounds like a real places to be, people to see kind of a situation, right?

158
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
This is resurrection day.

159
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
Everything that the Scriptures promised had been fulfilled.

160
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Jesus literally went to battle against the darkness of this world and won.

161
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:27,000
He rose from the grave if he had, I think he's got a few things to worry about, a few things to do, and yet he goes and he draws near to two of his friends who are walking away.

162
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,000
He's suffering and skeptical that anything he said has any reality in their life.

163
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,000
And I think this is an incredibly powerful truth for each of us to wrestle with today.

164
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
The God of the universe isn't too busy for you.

165
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
He isn't afraid of your anger and your questions.

166
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
He loves you.

167
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
He died for you.

168
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
He rose for you.

169
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:55,000
And he wants to invite you back to the place that he's been inviting you to all along.

170
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
This is such a Jesus move.

171
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:03,000
This is leaving the 99 to find the two.

172
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:27,000
Jesus, the resurrected Jesus, spends significant time on the most important day in human history, not marching into Rome to tell the powers and authorities that he conquered death and the grave, but marching beside two of his close friends who are walking away suffering and skeptical that everything they built their lives on is pointless.

173
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
As he draws near to them, they don't recognize him.

174
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
There's a lot of scholarly debate on exactly what is causing them not to recognize him, but I'm not smart enough to explain that to you this morning.

175
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
The point is they don't get it, right?

176
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
They don't understand what's happening.

177
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:47,000
And I think many of us who have suffered or wrestled with doubts can say the same thing.

178
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:53,000
In our deepest moments of suffering and doubt, we just could not see God at work.

179
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
And sometimes we get past it and we're able to look back in hindsight and we're able to say, oh, he was there all along.

180
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
He was present with me all along.

181
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:08,000
Sometimes people come into our lives that we love and that we trust and they come alongside of us when we can't see him, when we can't discern where he's at.

182
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:14,000
And they can tell us, they can see in our lives where he's working, but we can't see it for ourselves.

183
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
That's what these two followers are dealing with.

184
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:22,000
In their deepest time of suffering and doubt, they just couldn't see him at work.

185
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:26,000
So what does Jesus do next?

186
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Well, let's read it.

187
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
It's a real classic Jesus move here.

188
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:35,000
First classic Jesus move is leaving the 99 to see the two.

189
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Second classic Jesus move is asking a question he already knows the answer to.

190
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
Verse 17, he asked them, what are you discussing together as you walk along?

191
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
They stood still, their faces downcast.

192
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:57,000
One of them named Cleopas asked him, are you the only one visiting Jerusalem who does not know the things that have happened there in these last, in these few days?

193
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
What things?

194
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Jesus asked.

195
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
This is classic.

196
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,000
This is classic Jesus.

197
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
He asked the question he already knows the answer to.

198
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
And this isn't a waste of time.

199
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
It's not some cruel game he's trying to play.

200
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,000
It's not something he's trying to hold over his friend's head.

201
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:21,000
It's an opportunity for his friends to understand and articulate what's actually going on in their hearts.

202
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:29,000
It's an opportunity for them to say out loud the things that they're dealing with, the suffering that they're going through, the skepticism that they're wrestling with.

203
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:36,000
It's an opportunity for Jesus to allow them to hear the thing that he already knows is going on in their hearts and in their lives.

204
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:41,000
How many of you in this room would be brave enough to admit that you go to therapy?

205
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Therapy, double hands up for me.

206
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Therapy is great.

207
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:49,000
Counseling is great.

208
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,000
We all need it.

209
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
And if you think you don't, you need it even more than the rest of us do.

210
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:59,000
But Jesus is doing the thing that I know my therapist does to me.

211
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
When he asks me a question, I can tell the look in his eye.

212
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
When he asks me a question that he already knows the answer to.

213
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
I can see his face right now.

214
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
He gives me a little grin as he's asking a question that he knows the answer to.

215
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
But he needs me to articulate it.

216
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:26,000
He needs me to say out loud the things that are going inside in my heart and in my mind so he can confront me with truth and point out the flaws and the lies that I'm believing in my own heart and my own mind.

217
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,000
This is what Jesus is doing for his friends.

218
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:36,000
He's inviting them to articulate what's going on in their hearts and their minds so that he can confront them with what's actually true.

219
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
With what's actually real.

220
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:47,000
And their response clarifies the beliefs that are leading them on the road away from where God has invited them to.

221
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Verse 19.

222
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
About Jesus of Nazareth, they replied.

223
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
He was a prophet, powerful in word and in deed before God and all the people.

224
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:02,000
The chief priests and our rulers handed him over to be sentenced to death and they crucified him.

225
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,000
Listen to this.

226
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:07,000
This is their first admission of difficult things going on in their heart.

227
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:12,000
But we had hoped that he was going to be the one to redeem Israel.

228
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
And what is more, it is the third day since all this took place.

229
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
In addition, some of the women amazed us.

230
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:22,000
They went to the tomb early this morning, but they did not find his body.

231
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:26,000
They came and told us that they had seen a vision of angels who said he was alive.

232
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
Then some of our companions went to the tomb and found it just as the women said, evidence, right?

233
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Two different groups of people seeing an empty tomb.

234
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:36,000
But what did they say?

235
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:40,000
But they did not see Jesus.

236
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:48,000
I think this response clearly demonstrates two primary underlying beliefs leading them away from God.

237
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:54,000
And I think it's two underlying beliefs that many of us have struggled with today and in the past as well.

238
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,000
We had hoped.

239
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:59,000
This is an acknowledgement of suffering loss.

240
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:04,000
The death of Jesus signified the death of their future hope.

241
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,000
They're suffering the loss of a life that they had hoped for.

242
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:12,000
Things didn't work out the way that they thought it would.

243
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:15,000
And they gave up everything to follow Jesus.

244
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
And now they're sad, confused, frustrated, and disillusioned.

245
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:27,000
Suffering is leading them away from the place God's called them to be.

246
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:36,000
And I can't help but think many of us who have walked away from God or are walking away from God right now are doing so because suffering has come into our life.

247
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,000
We had hoped.

248
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
We had hoped.

249
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:43,000
But God doesn't seem to be showing up the way that we thought he would.

250
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:46,000
If he's all good and all powerful, why doesn't he intervene?

251
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Why doesn't he show up in this situation?

252
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Why doesn't he do the thing we thought he would do?

253
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:57,000
We had hoped that he was going to redeem us from our enemies.

254
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Second, again, they have two evidences of the resurrection, two different groups of people seeing an empty tomb.

255
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,000
These followers were likely intimately aware of Jesus' statements.

256
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:14,000
He made multiple statements as he was walking closer to Jerusalem that he would have to suffer, be crucified, and raised from the dead.

257
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
They likely heard those claims.

258
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:17,000
And now they're with a group of people.

259
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
Two different groups of people go and find the empty tomb.

260
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
But they're still skeptical.

261
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:26,000
They end their statement to Jesus by saying they did not see him.

262
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
They just need a little bit more evidence.

263
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
They just need him to show up one more time.

264
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:37,000
They just need to see his hands and his feet before they'll believe.

265
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Their suffering led to skepticism.

266
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,000
Even after being confronted with the empty tomb, they're still not able to believe.

267
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
And again, I think many of us can resonate with this progression.

268
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:51,000
We put our hope in God.

269
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
Suffering has come our way.

270
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
And the thing we put our hope in has apparently let us down.

271
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:00,000
It leads us to question God's existence altogether or at least his goodness.

272
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
How many of us can resonate with this story?

273
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
We had hoped.

274
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:14,000
But what if we're hoping in the wrong thing?

275
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:20,000
What if our skepticism in God's goodness is because we've defined good and evil on our own?

276
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:25,000
And we've wanted him to do a thing that he never promised or said he was going to do.

277
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:29,000
And he's been faithful to do the thing that he promised and said he was going to do.

278
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:34,000
But we've ignored that because we've redefined something that we want him to do that he never said he would do.

279
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,000
Verse 26.

280
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:41,000
We'll start with 25.

281
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:44,000
He said to them, "How foolish you are.

282
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:47,000
How slow to believe all the prophets had spoken.

283
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Did not the Messiah have to suffer these things and then enter his glory?

284
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
And beginning with Moses and all the prophets,

285
00:20:54,000 --> 00:21:03,000
he explained to them what was said in all of the Scriptures concerning himself."

286
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,000
Just like my counselor.

287
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,000
Asked me a question.

288
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:14,000
Invites me to state what I believe is true, what's leading to my actions.

289
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:21,000
Jesus invites his friends to give him an understanding of why they're walking away from the place that he's invited them to.

290
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
They articulate it.

291
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:24,000
It's suffering.

292
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:25,000
It's skepticism.

293
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:29,000
And Jesus lovingly rebukes them and corrects them with truth.

294
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
And I think we can't miss this either.

295
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:39,000
We live in a culture that wants to redefine the love of Jesus for us as one that affirms all of our desires and our actions.

296
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:43,000
Even if they're harmful to ourselves or others.

297
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:47,000
We want a Jesus who just pats us on the back and says it's going to be okay.

298
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:55,000
But sometimes Jesus loves us so much that he's not willing to let us go down a path that's leading to our destruction.

299
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:03,000
Sometimes he lovingly rebukes us and invites us to see that what we're doing is not leading to the abundant life that he promised us.

300
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:09,000
And the other thing that I love about Jesus, if you're here, you're newer to Christianity, you're newer to faith,

301
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:13,000
and you're like, whoa, whoa, whoa, I wanted the Jesus that was lovingly affirming everything.

302
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
Like I thought, I've read some Jesus stuff.

303
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,000
He seems loving and affirming.

304
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
What is this, you foolish people, slow of heart?

305
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
I didn't know Jesus was like that.

306
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:26,000
What I want you to hear this morning is that Jesus throughout his teachings and his life and his ministry,

307
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:31,000
the closer proximity people have to his teaching and understanding,

308
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:36,000
the closer proximity they have to the temple and the Old Testament law, the harder his rebukes are.

309
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:42,000
And so if you're here and you don't know much about Jesus at all and you hear that rebuke and you're like, oh man,

310
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Jesus is not as cool or nice or loving as I thought he was.

311
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:51,000
Jesus will rebuke you, but for those of you who are far off, it's very gentle and loving at first.

312
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:56,000
It's those of us who have grown up in the church, those of us who have been exposed to the Scriptures,

313
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
those of us who have learned things of Jesus.

314
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:05,000
When we disobey, when we run in the opposite direction, it's still loving, but it's a bit more firm.

315
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
Like, guys, I told you this multiple times.

316
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
I was going to suffer, die and be raised from the dead.

317
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:10,000
You were there.

318
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:11,000
You missed it.

319
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
Now I'm here and you still missed it.

320
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:14,000
I love you, but figure it out.

321
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:22,000
So he rebukes them in love and then he corrects them.

322
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:26,000
And I call this in verse 27, the greatest sermon never recorded.

323
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:32,000
We don't really hear anything that he says other than from the beginning,

324
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:35,000
from the books of Moses through the prophets and all the Scriptures,

325
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:39,000
all of the Old Testament, he's walking them through a sermon about how the whole entire

326
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:43,000
Old Testament from Genesis to the end of, where are we at?

327
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:44,000
Malachi?

328
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:45,000
Did I figure that out?

329
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:46,000
Or Micah?

330
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:47,000
Malachi.

331
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:48,000
All right.

332
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:49,000
Bible college degree.

333
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
I panicked here for a second.

334
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:52,000
It's all about him.

335
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
Jesus walks them through the Scriptures.

336
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
They have been taught and memorized since childhood.

337
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:59,000
And he explains to them, they all clearly point to him.

338
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:04,000
Jesus is the seed of the woman promised in Genesis 3 who would crush the head of the serpent.

339
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:09,000
He's the faithful descendant of Abraham in Genesis 12 who will be a blessing to all nations.

340
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
He's the Passover lamb in Exodus.

341
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
The prophet to come after Moses in Deuteronomy.

342
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
The promised descendant of King David who would have an everlasting throne.

343
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
The eternal Son of Man from the book of Daniel.

344
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:22,000
And on and on he went and on and on we could go.

345
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,000
All of the Scriptures point to the person and work of Jesus.

346
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
Hebrews chapter one.

347
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:33,000
Long ago, God spoke many times and in many ways to our ancestors through the prophets.

348
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:37,000
And now in these final days, he has spoken to us through his son.

349
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:40,000
God promised everything to the son as an inheritance.

350
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
And through the son, he created the universe.

351
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:46,000
The son radiates God's own glory and expresses the very character of God.

352
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:49,000
And he sustains everything by the mighty power of his command.

353
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:56,000
And when he had cleansed us from our sins, he sat down in the place of honor at the right hand of God in heaven.

354
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:03,000
Oh, to be a fly on the butt of a donkey.

355
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:04,000
To hear this sermon.

356
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:07,000
This sermon.

357
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:12,000
All of it points to Jesus.

358
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:17,000
The one who would crush the very power of sin in the grave.

359
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:21,000
And establish an eternal kingdom that would be a blessing to the entire world.

360
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:26,000
These disciples were suffering and skeptical because their hope was in the wrong thing.

361
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:31,000
They wanted physical and earthly deliverance from their perceived enemies, the Roman.

362
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:34,000
Sound familiar?

363
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:44,000
Are any of us suffering and skeptical about God because we're waiting for him to provide temporary earthly blessings?

364
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:48,000
We're waiting for him to show up and do a thing that we thought he would do.

365
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:52,000
Show up and redeem a thing that he thought he would redeem.

366
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:59,000
Are we possibly putting expectations on Jesus that he never came to fulfill?

367
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:04,000
They were waiting for the Messiah to deliver them from Rome.

368
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:07,000
But the problem is the Romans aren't the real enemies.

369
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
Jesus knows the true enemy isn't Rome.

370
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:15,000
It's the spiritual forces working behind Rome and behind all other powerful influences of evil and injustice.

371
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:18,000
If he defeats Rome, another evil will rise up again.

372
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:24,000
Because behind all evil empires and behind all evil principles, there are spiritual forces.

373
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:30,000
The same spiritual forces that deceived Adam and Eve and ushered in the reign of death in God's good world.

374
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:34,000
And in order for God to fully redeem creation, Jesus would have to defeat death itself.

375
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:37,000
And this is what makes the gospel good news.

376
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:43,000
Not just for first century Jews, but for the entire world for all time.

377
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:44,000
Our enemy has been defeated.

378
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:49,000
Eternal life in the future and abundant life now is available to anyone who would have eyes to see.

379
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:53,000
The spirit of God whom raised Jesus from the dead is available to us.

380
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:59,000
Giving us power to defeat sin in our lives and giving us a hope that goes far beyond the grave.

381
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:06,000
They're walking away because they put their hope in the wrong thing.

382
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
Because they want a temporary deliverance.

383
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:11,000
When Jesus came to provide eternal deliverance.

384
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:14,000
They want a temporary deliverance for one group of people at one time.

385
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:20,000
And Jesus came to bring eternal deliverance for all groups of people who would place their faith in him at all times.

386
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:30,000
I try to make this sermon shorter than normal because I'm confident that whatever Jesus preached is far better than anything I'm going to preach.

387
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
And they still didn't get it.

388
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Which means there's little hope for you with me up here on the stage.

389
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:39,000
So we'll try and keep it short.

390
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:42,000
He moves on from there.

391
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:45,000
The gentle rebuke and the loving correction.

392
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:46,000
Verse 28.

393
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:49,000
As they approached the village to which they were going.

394
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:52,000
I don't know how long it takes you to walk seven miles.

395
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:55,000
But it sounds like that's about how long Jesus' sermon was.

396
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
So you're welcome.

397
00:27:57,000 --> 00:28:03,000
Jesus continued as if he was going on further.

398
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:04,000
But they urged him strongly.

399
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:05,000
Stay with us.

400
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:06,000
For it is near evening.

401
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:07,000
The day is almost over.

402
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
So he went in to stay with them.

403
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:12,000
When he was at the table with them, he took bread.

404
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:15,000
He gave thanks.

405
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:19,000
He broke it and began giving it to them.

406
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:22,000
Then their eyes were open.

407
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
They recognized him.

408
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:27,000
And he disappeared from their sight.

409
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:33,000
They asked each other, were not our hearts burning within us while he talked with us on the road and opened the Scriptures to us?

410
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:36,000
They got up and returned at once repentance.

411
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:44,000
They are going back to the place God invited them to be immediately without hesitation, without any care or concern that it's almost dark and it's probably not the safest journey.

412
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:50,000
They are running back to where they have been invited to be because God showed up in their life.

413
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:59,000
John the eleven and those with them assembled together and saying, it is true, the Lord has raised and he has appeared to Simon.

414
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:02,000
Then the two told what had happened on the way.

415
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:11,000
How Jesus in the most important day in human history, the real places to be, people to see situation, he showed up to us as we were walking away.

416
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:16,000
He shows up to you and to me.

417
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:21,000
Does he have more important things to do?

418
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:22,000
Probably.

419
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:28,000
But his love, his grace, his compassion extends towards you and towards me.

420
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:38,000
They told them what had happened on the way and how they had recognized Jesus when he broke the bread.

421
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:42,000
Jesus moves from their head into their heart.

422
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:45,000
He lifts the bread and he blesses it and he breaks it.

423
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:52,000
The very symbol he established just prior to his crucifixion as the primary symbol of the new covenant.

424
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:56,000
Band, you guys can come on up.

425
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:57,000
We're getting close.

426
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:01,000
A symbol that is steeped in Jewish history.

427
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:06,000
A clear representation of the lengths that Jesus went through to provide salvation for us.

428
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:19,000
And at a combination of hearing God's word proclaimed and seeing the symbol of God's word broken in front of them, it awakened their eyes to see Jesus for who he truly was.

429
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:31,000
Not just the promised Messiah, but the very word of God who became flesh and whose flesh was broken, not to set Israel free from their physical enemies, but to set the whole world free from our spiritual enemies.

430
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:35,000
To proclaim victory over sin, death in the grave.

431
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:36,000
In the grave.

432
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:37,000
1 Corinthians 15.

433
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,000
O death, where is your victory?

434
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
O death, where is your sting?

435
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:46,000
For sin is the sting that results in death and the law gives sin its power.

436
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:52,000
But thank God he gives us victory over sin and death through our Lord Jesus Christ.

437
00:30:53,000 --> 00:31:01,000
My main point this morning, my main answer to the question that we began with, what does Jesus do for those of us who are walking away suffering and skeptical?

438
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:08,000
Jesus draws near to the suffering and the skeptical with an invitation of hope rooted in the resurrection.

439
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:13,000
It's not an invitation to blind faith.

440
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:18,000
It's rooted in the evidence of Scripture from Genesis to Revelation.

441
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:19,000
It's all about Jesus.

442
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:25,000
It's head, intellect, and heart, emotion.

443
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:28,000
It's back to the Old Testament.

444
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:32,000
Love the Lord your God with all of your heart, soul, mind, and strength, and love your neighbor as yourself.

445
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:38,000
It's a singular unified narrative of the Bible woven over thousands of years and multiple authors.

446
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:43,000
Not only is it rooted in scriptural evidence, it's rooted in historical evidence.

447
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:47,000
Take a look at the claims of the resurrection for yourself.

448
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,000
Jesus isn't inviting you to blind faith.

449
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:52,000
He's given you all that you need to see him.

450
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:55,000
Will you look?

451
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:56,000
Will you knock?

452
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:57,000
Will you seek?

453
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:58,000
Will you ask?

454
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:02,000
Will you allow your suffering to lead to skepticism?

455
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:05,000
And will you walk away without ever giving him a chance?

456
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:15,000
If you have a bulletin, there should be a QR code in there.

457
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:17,000
I'd invite you to take that home.

458
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:22,000
That is a video series, short videos, five minutes a piece.

459
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:26,000
And it's really a primer on how to read the Scriptures.

460
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:28,000
How to see Jesus throughout the Scriptures.

461
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:32,000
And so if you're here and you're searching and you want to give Jesus a shot.

462
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:35,000
You want to see what some of the things he might have talked about in his sermon.

463
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,000
His seven mile long sermon.

464
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:40,000
I'd invite you to take that QR code home and check that out.

465
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:44,000
Check it out with somebody that you know, that reads the Bible, that you can trust.

466
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:47,000
If you don't have anybody like that, reach out to us.

467
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:51,000
We'll gladly partner you with somebody who will read the Scriptures with you.

468
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:54,000
And let you see Jesus all throughout the pages of it.

469
00:32:54,000 --> 00:33:01,000
The resurrection of Jesus happened, it demands a response.

470
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:05,000
Will we close our eyes and walk away?

471
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:08,000
Or will we look to him whose body was broken for us?

472
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:12,000
And find victory and hope in the midst of our suffering and skepticism.

473
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,000
If you're here and you're suffering.

474
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:19,000
If a lack of hope has led you to be skeptical of the person and work of Jesus.

475
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
I just want you to know that God loves you.

476
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:25,000
That this story is an example of what he wants to do for you.

477
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:26,000
He wants to draw near to you.

478
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:30,000
He wants to confront you lovingly with the lies you are believing.

479
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:33,000
And he wants to lead you into truth.

480
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:38,000
Truth that will provide abundant life now and eternal life in the future.

481
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:43,000
I invite you to search the Scriptures for yourself and community with others.

482
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:49,000
And I invite you to stay connected to a local church that prioritizes the bread and the cup.

483
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:54,000
These are the two symbols that Jesus confronts these people with.

484
00:33:54,000 --> 00:34:00,000
His word and his body broken and shed.

485
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:05,000
My guess is the same thing will begin to happen to you as you search the Scriptures.

486
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
Your heart will burn within you.

487
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:10,000
It's too obvious not to see it.

488
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:11,000
If you really give it a chance.

489
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:16,000
And my hope is that as you stay connected to a local church.

490
00:34:16,000 --> 00:34:20,000
That one day as the symbol is passed out.

491
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:23,000
Your eyes will be open just like the disciples.
